        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Nubians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>; today Aswan Governorate in Egypt, especially Kom Ombo and Nasr al-Nuba, and Sudan’s Northern State from Wadi Halfa to al Dabbah near the Fourth Cataract, with many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>; also the Midob and Birgid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and the Hill Nubians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a>; ancient centres <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements from 7000 BC; the A-Group (3700–2800 BC), C-Group (2300–1600 BC) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> (2500–1500 BC) cultures; the Twenty-fifth Dynasty (744–656 BC); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> (to 300/350 AD); the Christian kingdoms of Nobatia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a> (4th century to 1504); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> by the 16th century; displacement by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> from the 1960s</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Nubian languages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-nubian">Old Nubian</a>, written from the 8th century AD; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> famed in antiquity (“<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-the-bow">Land of the Bow</a>”); the Twenty-fifth Dynasty that ruled all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; the pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>, Nuri, El Kerru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>; the frescoes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt treaty</a>; the Nubian vault; the Sebu birth ceremony</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Egyptian texts, Greek authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Meroitic inscriptions and Greek inscriptions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silko">Silko</a>’s at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalabsha">Kalabsha</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-nubian">Old Nubian</a> documents; archaeology from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qustul">Qustul</a> to Kulubnarti; linguistic work by Claude Rilly, Julien Cooper and others; genetic studies of ancient and living Nubians</span></div>
