    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Nubians are indigenous to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley between southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements date back to 7000 BC, and they speak Nilo-Saharan Nubian languages with Arabic as a second language.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> culture, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> and the Twenty-fifth Dynasty, under which all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was united with Nubia from 744 to 656 BC.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>, built from the 1960s, flooded much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> and displaced 100,000 Nubians, many of them forcibly relocated to New Halfa, Khashm el Girba and “New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>” near Kom Ombo.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur-war">Yom Kippur War</a> of 1973, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> used Nubians as code talkers.</p></aside>
    </section>

