    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 7000 BC</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements appear in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, with Wadi Halfa believed to be the oldest settlement of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">744 BC</span><span class="rf-text">The Twenty-fifth Dynasty unites all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BC</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> records the Noubai west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> as a numerous people divided into several kingdoms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">4th century AD</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> disintegrates and gives way to Nobatia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">651</span><span class="rf-text">Makuria repulses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>’s invasion and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt treaty</a> is signed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">8th century AD</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-nubian">Old Nubian</a> is first attested in writing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">16th century</span><span class="rf-text">Most Nubians have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1960s</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> floods much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> and displaces about 100,000 Nubians</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The prehistory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, around 300,000 years ago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements in central Nubia date to 7000 BC, Wadi Halfa being believed the oldest in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley, and by about 6000 BC its peoples farmed. Archaeologists divide its ancient history into the A-Group (3700–2800 BC), C-Group (2300–1600 BC) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> (2500–1500 BC) cultures, followed by the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. Biological studies show close affinities between the early Nubians and the predynastic peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Bruce Williams argued that Nubian elites took part with the early Egyptian rulers in shaping pharaonic civilisation.</p><p>The languages of early Nubia are debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-behrens">Peter Behrens</a> and Marianne Bechhaus-Gerst suggested the C-Group and Kerma peoples spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Cushitic languages; Claude Rilly and Julien Cooper argue that Kerma spoke an Eastern Sudanic language, while Cushitic was spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>. The first speakers of Nubian languages, another branch of Eastern Sudanic, came later.</p><p>The name comes from the Noba. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a>, in the 3rd century BC, described the Noubai as a numerous people west of the Nile; Rilly links the word to Meroitic nob, used for the people and for “slave”. By the 5th century AD the Noba used the name themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> had four kinds of land: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley in the north and centre, farmed by irrigation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a>, with peasant farming and nomadism; eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, mostly nomadic; and a fertile pastoral south where the larger farming communities lived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> were its largest towns in antiquity. Kings came from clans that controlled the gold mines, and Gamal Mokhtar noted that Nubians relied more than Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> on mobile stock-raising.</p><p>Today the main Nile Nubian groups, from north to south, are the Kenzi, who speak Kenzi or Mattokki, the Faddicca, Halfawi, Sukkot and Mahas, who speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a>, and the Danagla, who speak Andaandi. Most Nubians now work in the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Sudan. Arabic, once learned mainly by men who travelled for work, is increasingly learned by women through school, radio and television, and more Nubian women work outside the home.</p><p>The Halfawis, Mahas and Danagla write with their own script and keep traditions such as Sebu, a ceremony for a birth. Scarification was once practised, three scars on each cheek among the Mahas and on the temples among the Danagla, but younger generations have abandoned it. Nubians share a common identity celebrated in poetry, novels, music and storytelling.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nubians today are Muslims, and their practice mixes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with traditional folk beliefs to some degree. In ancient times they followed a mixture of Kushite and Egyptian religion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> was a great religious centre: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>, a sandstone hill that its people saw as a rearing cobra, was declared by Egyptian priests to be the home of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, and Egyptian and Nubian gods were worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> for 2,500 years. Nubian kings and queens were buried in pyramids at Jebel Barkal, Nuri, El Kerru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>.</p><p>After the fall of Kush, missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> competed in Nubia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Church</a> established its authority. The kingdoms of Nobatia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a> were Christian, and frescoes painted between 800 and 1200 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> and other cities show the religious life of their courts.</p><p>Islam arrived from the 8th century, first with Arab merchants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt treaty</a> of 651 required the Nubians to keep a mosque for Muslim visitors. Conversion began with the elite and was carried mainly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> preachers who settled in Nubia from the late 14th century; by the 16th century most Nubians were Muslims.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> tꜣ-stj, the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-the-bow">Land of the Bow</a>”, and its warriors were famed for their skill with the bow and arrow. Nubian kings of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty were regarded as pious scholars and patrons of the arts, copying old Egyptian texts and restoring Egyptian practices, and their pyramids still stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>, Nuri, El Kerru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. Frank Yurco noted that royal crowns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> falcons and victory scenes were concentrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and the A-Group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>.</p><p>The Christian kingdoms wrote and painted in a style drawn from Byzantine Greek models. Greek served as a written language and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>, taking words from Coptic and Nubian, and royal titles followed Byzantine models: an inscription names “the great King Moses [son] of Georgios, the King of the Nobatians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>, Makuria”. The frescoes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a>, painted between 800 and 1200, are in the Byzantine style. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-nubian">Old Nubian</a>, written from the 8th century AD, may be the oldest well-recorded African language outside the Afroasiatic family.</p><p>Nubians invented the Nubian vault, a curved vaulted structure. Modern Nubian houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> have a large walled courtyard and an ornate gate, preferably facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, with bright stucco painted with family symbols, palm trees, geometric patterns or the evil eye.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> has been called “the corridor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>”, where Nubians met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory</a>, animal skins and other goods of Africa passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Egyptian rulers sought its gold and trade routes, dominating Nubia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. In turn Nubian rulers controlled Egypt for a century, and the Twenty-fifth Dynasty united Egypt with Nubia from 744 BC until the native Twenty-sixth Dynasty took Egypt back in 656 BC.</p><p>In the 4th century AD Kush disintegrated under worsening climate, rebellion and invasion by the Noba, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumites</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> and looted its gold. In the Christian era the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> invaded Makuria in 651 and was repulsed twice, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt treaty</a> that followed prevented further Arab invasions in exchange for 360 slaves a year. The Ottoman occupation of Lower Nubia in the 1560s and the Funj Sultanate’s annexation of the lands south of the Third Cataract by the early 16th century ended Christian Nubia.</p><p>Nubia then came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the Muhammad Ali dynasty and British colonial rule, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>’s independence in 1956 the Nubians were divided between Egypt and Sudan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nubians still live in the land of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and in the cities of both countries. The building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> from the 1960s flooded much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, their ancestral land. It displaced 100,000 Nubians in Egypt and Sudan: an estimated 50,000 Halfawi Nubians were forcibly relocated from Wadi Halfa to Khashm el Girba and New Halfa in Sudan, and the Faddicca and Kenzi of Egypt were forcibly moved to villages of “New Nubia” near Kom Ombo.</p><p>Genetic studies find that Nubians cluster with the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley to the north and with their Afroasiatic-speaking neighbours, and that Christian-era Nubians of Kulubnarti already carried West Eurasian ancestry before the Arab migrations, reflecting long ties along the river.</p><p>The Nubian languages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a>, Kenzi and Andaandi, are still spoken as mother tongues, and Nubian musicians such as Hamza El Din, Mohammed Wardi and Mohamed Mounir and writers such as Idris Ali carry the culture forward. Nubians have used their languages in public life too: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur-war">Yom Kippur War</a> of 1973 Egypt employed Nubian code talkers.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Nubians are the indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley between southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, heirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, Kush and the Christian kingdoms of Nobatia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>. Muslims since the 16th century, they speak Nubian languages, and many were forcibly moved when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> flooded much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> in the 1960s.</em></p>

